Project-local source-backed packages are scoped `@package.moth` support packages discovered structurally from the project source tree. Structural discovery determines package roots without configuration.

### Directory structure

[codeblock, $code "text":
    project-root/
    ├── @config.moth
    ├── @entry.moth
    ├── markdown/
    │   ├── @package.moth
    │   ├── parser/
    │   │   └── @mod.moth
    │   └── rendering/
    │       └── @mod.moth
    ├── pages/
    │   └── @mod.moth
    └── @package.moth
]

The `markdown` directory contains an `@package.moth` root, making `@markdown` a scoped support package visible to the entry root, `@pages` and their descendants.

### Scoped visibility

For a support package `S` whose nearest ancestor normal-position module is `P`:

- `S` is visible to `P`
- `S` is visible to normal sibling modules of `S` and their descendants
- `S` is not visible above `P`
- `S` is not visible outside `P`'s subtree
- `S` is not visible inside its own private implementation subtree
- another support package in the same owner scope cannot depend on `S`

A module with `@mod.moth` or `@entry.moth` occupies the normal-position owner role. An entry-only module can therefore own support descendants even though it publishes no normal API. A co-located `@entry.moth` gives no entry privileges to its `@package.moth` facade.

### Public surface

Consumers see only declarations and direct-selection re-exports marked with `$export` in the support facade. Internal implementation files remain private unless the facade re-exports selected declarations.

Shared helpers in a paired directory must obey the intersection of imports legal to every root that can consume them.

### Private subtrees

The support facade may depend on ordinary files it owns, any descendant module in its private subtree, support packages from a strictly outer scope and registered packages. It may not depend on its parent, normal sibling consumers or same-scope support siblings.

Consumers see only the support facade's `$export` surface. They cannot address `@markdown/parser` or another implementation path.

### Depended-on package roots do not produce routes

Support roots are API-only. They reject top-level runtime work, direct fragments and builder-slot contributions, export declarations instead and produce no HTML artefact. Only `@entry.moth` creates a page. Importing a support package never activates an entry in its directory. See @../project-structure/#html-routing-and-artifacts (HTML routing and artefacts).

Support packages may hold reusable ordinary `#` constants and expose them through their public surface. They do not need custom project metadata. Project metadata remains the separate `@config.moth` contract.

### Namespace limits and copied packages

Local support packages stay outside the reserved Core and Builder namespaces. An importable local facade, helper or dependency alias cannot claim `core` or `builder`, compared case-insensitively. A directory name alone never triggers that rejection: entry-only modules, their private helpers, directories containing only entry modules and plain asset directories remain legal. Unrelated `html/`, `css/`, `styles/` and `web/` folders also stay legal.

A project folder whose full path matches a registered source package path, such as `core/html/` or `builder/web/site/`, collides whichever module owns it, even when it holds no importable surface. An ancestor folder such as `core/` alone stays legal. A binding-backed package has no source module, so an asset folder at a registered binding path such as `builder/web/canvas/` stays legal.

Copying a source-backed package directory to a non-colliding local path gives an ordinary support package through its own `@package.moth`. Copying it to a registered source package path, such as `core/html/`, hits the collision above. Copying a binding-backed asset tree creates no package and registers no symbols. Neither copy inherits the registered package's origin, namespace or capabilities.

### Origin and backing are separate

Scoped `@package.moth` packages are ProjectLocal packages backed by Moth source. Registered MothSource packages also publish through a directly contained `@package.moth`, while `@mod.moth` alone is insufficient for registration. `@core/html` and `@core/css` are source-backed with Core origin. `@core/math` and `@builder/web/canvas` are binding-backed packages with Core and Builder origins respectively. See @#package-origins-and-backing (Package origins and backing).

### Concepts related to project-local packages

- For module root rules see @../project-structure/#module-roots (Module roots).
- For `$export` rules see @../project-structure/#public-api (Public API).
- For dependency path resolution see @#dependency-paths (Dependency paths).
